---
name: review-delivery
description: Read before acting on wait-mate, reviewing, merging or closing a Crew.
---

## 9. Supervision, review and delivery

**You never wait for a Crew.**
Not with `sleep`, not with a loop, not by re-running `state` or `peek`: a command that waits locks the captain out for as long as it waits, and a Crew's news reaches you as a digest (section 10) the moment it is written.
A `⟦mate⟧` line wakes you when a Crew needs you: run `/usr/local/bin/mate wake shop` once, first, and act on what it prints (section 10); do not tail status files, cat hand-backs or run `state` to rediscover it, and `peek` only a `blocked` Crew, then load `stuck-crew-recovery` rather than waiting it out.
Do not report routine, unremarkable wakes to the captain; batch anything worth mentioning into your next natural reply to them.

**On `wait-mate:`.** The Crew has stopped - which can mean "it is done" or "I could not, here is why". Do not relay it to the captain as a result. Load `decision-authority`, then verify it, in this order:

1. Start from the `wait-mate` item `wake` printed: the branch summary, the hand-back or report path and the reviews recorded for it. Peek nothing.
2. Reconstruct the contract from `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews/<id>/brief.md`: `## Captain's words`, including anything appended since spawn, then `## Build` and `## Acceptance`.
   Never from the Crew's own account of what it was asked.
3. On a ship, read `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews/<id>/handback.md` against that contract before anything else: every line of `## Acceptance` has a row, each row says `pass` with a command and output that actually show it, `Deviations from Build` holds nothing the captain's words did not ask for, and `Still open` holds nothing that was the task's to settle.
   A missing hand-back is a one-line send asking for it, not a reason to read the diff instead.
4. Start an independent reviewer: `/usr/local/bin/mate review shop <id> --id <new-review-id>`. Without `--model` and `--effort` it runs the dispatch table's review profile; pass `--harness` to choose the reviewer's harness, and a full `--harness --model --effort` profile only for a review the table gives its own rule, such as a large change (`crew-dispatch` section 9). Then end this turn; `wake` lists the review under this hand-back when it lands. The reviewer reads the full committed diff, original brief and acceptance evidence in its own context. Its tokens remain in the Crew ledger.
   When the reviewer hands back, read `mate report shop <review-id> --summary` and then `mate review shop <id> --check <review-id>`. A stale SHA/base/brief/hand-back, incomplete verdict or unresolved finding requires correction or a new review. A failed check never becomes approval. Read the detailed report sections when the summary is insufficient.
   Direct review is still available when the captain requests it: `mate diff shop <id> --full`. Read the entire diff and verify the evidence; default `mate diff` is only a stat and cannot approve work.
5. On a scout, start with `mate report shop <id> --summary`, then read the evidence and relevant detailed sections needed to verify its claims; a scout commits nothing, so its diff is empty and the report is the whole deliverable.

Read-only git against the Crew's worktree is allowed where you need something `mate diff` does not print, and only in read-only forms; a git that writes, stages, commits, checks out, or merges is forbidden by rule 1 of section 1 - landing a branch is `mate merge` and nothing else.
A correction inside the contract is yours: a `fail` row, a criterion with no row, a deviation `## Build` did not allow, a diff that does not do what the captain's words ask - send the Crew one corrective line naming it.
The smallest downstream changes needed to keep accepted behavior correct, add a test for it, or keep documentation accurate stay inside the contract; a new requirement is follow-up work, not part of this task.
A correction that would expand the contract, or a product call the captain's words do not settle, goes to the captain instead (section 13), and the Crew waits.
When a line tells the Crew to wait for something - a check, a build, another branch - it waits once, with one blocking command and a long timeout; never a sleep loop, never "check again every few minutes".

**The fix after a review** is checked from the SHA that review covered, never from scratch: a full re-review of a two-line fix was measured at 2.2M tokens.
Your own read is read-only git in the Crew's worktree: `git diff <reviewed-sha>..HEAD --stat`, and after a rebase or a merge of the default branch `git range-diff <base>..<reviewed-sha> <base>..HEAD`.
`mate merge --review` pins the head and base the reviewer saw, so a branch that moved still needs a review recorded at its new head; what you decide is how much that review does:

- Docs only, a few lines that touch no code, or a merge of the default branch whose `range-diff` shows no semantic change: you have checked it yourself. Start `mate review` as usual and in the same turn send the reviewer one line, `/usr/local/bin/mate send shop <review-id> "Reviewed at <sha>; since then only <docs | a merge of <default> with no semantic change>: confirm with git diff <sha>..HEAD and hand back; rerun nothing" --queue`.
- A fix that touches code: the reviewer gets the range and the findings, nothing more. `mate review` has no `--since` yet, so start it as usual and send one line the same way: `"Reviewed at <sha>; re-check only: <findings>; diff <sha>..HEAD, and rerun a gate only when the change touches its files or commands"`.
- Two rounds of review at most. A third correction on the same theme is the captain's call, as `decision-authority` says: report the evidence and your recommendation, and the Crew waits.

**Delivery.** You chose how this ship lands when you spawned it (`--deliver`, in its meta as `delivery`). Once the review passes, you land it yourself with one command:

```text
/usr/local/bin/mate merge shop <id> --review <review-id>
```

The app refuses your merge without a passing review, and pins it to the commit the reviewer saw, so a branch or contract that moved after review is refused with `review is stale`.
A successful merge closes the Crew, so you do not also run `crew stop`; report the one line it printed, in outcome language, and stop.
When memory `## Captain` says the captain merges this kind of work, report it as ready instead - naming the branch or the pull request - and they merge it from their console or on GitHub.

**A local ship** (`delivery=local`) is fast-forwarded onto the default branch of the primary checkout.
If the merge answers `merge refused: needs-rebase: ...`, the default branch has moved on and the branch is no longer a fast-forward.
Nothing changed and the Crew is exactly as it was, so send it one line - `/usr/local/bin/mate send shop <id> "rebase onto <default> and hand back with wait-mate"`, with that default branch in place of `<default>` - and merge again after its next `wait-mate`.
`needs-rebase` is never a state of the Crew; it is the answer to a command you ran.
Section 4's "Delivery" lists the other refusals and what each one asks of you.

**A pull request ship** (`delivery=pr`) has pushed its branch and opened one: `pr-open:` is its hand-back line, the URL is in `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews/<id>.status` and in its meta as `pr_url`, and a watcher follows it. What you review is that branch, exactly as above. A correction after review goes to the Crew as one line; it fixes the branch and pushes, and the same pull request follows. `mate merge` runs `gh pr merge` for it; pushing and opening are the Crew's.
If a Crew names a pull request URL in its status any other way, the console starts the watcher for it; `/usr/local/bin/mate pr watch shop <id> <url>` starts it yourself when no console is open.

**When a pull request ends.** Whoever merges it, the watcher notices within a minute, fast-forwards the default branch of the primary checkout when that checkout is clean and on it, records `pr_state` and `merge_commit` in the Crew's meta, appends `pr-merged: <url>` or `pr-closed: <url>` to its status file, and types one `⟦mate⟧ pr-merged:` or `pr-closed:` line into your pane, in manual mode too (`event-handling` shows the line).

On `pr-merged:` run `/usr/local/bin/mate crew stop shop <id>`: it takes the merged pull request as landed - also after a squash or rebase merge, where the branch is not in the default branch's history - removes the Crew's worktree and branch, and records it `finished`. Then do the next task in the backlog. If the line says the checkout was skipped, tell the captain in one line, because their copy of the default branch is behind.
On `pr-closed:` the pull request was closed without being merged. Find out why (its comments, through a scout if they are long), then decide: send the Crew the rework and have it reopen the pull request (`gh pr reopen`) and run `pr watch` again, or tell the captain the work was turned down. Throwing the branch away (`--discard`) waits for the captain's word.

**Closing a Crew.** A Crew's task ends when you or the captain decide it has, never when the Crew says so.
`wait-mate:` is the Crew's report; closing is `/usr/local/bin/mate crew stop shop <id>`, and until it runs the Crew stays in the captain's console and in `crew list` as work in flight.

- A **scout** is closed when its report answers every question under its `## Deliverable` and you have relayed the findings. Keep it open while you still need a follow-up answered by the same Crew, with its context intact.
- A **ship** is closed when its work has landed: its branch merged into the default branch of its repo, or its pull request merged (`pr-merged:`). When you merged it with `mate merge`, it is already closed. When the captain merged it, run `crew stop`; it will find the work landed and record the Crew `finished`.
- A Crew whose unlanded work is thrown away is closed with `--discard`, on the captain's explicit word only. That records it `failed`.

Closing removes the Crew from the console and from `crew list`; `crews/<id>/` with the brief and any report stays, and `crew list --all` still shows the record.
Record accepted delivery and update any linked external task through the captain's configured task workflow; a handback or discarded Crew alone does not close the task.
A Crew that went quiet or said `wait-mate` is a hand-back to review, not a reason to close it.
